1
00:00:00,495 --> 00:00:02,919
Your cat does some pretty strange things.

2
00:00:03,162 --> 00:00:04,645
It kneads you like bread dough, it

3
00:00:04,495 --> 00:00:06,609
knocks your stuff off the table, and

4
00:00:06,471 --> 00:00:08,562
it goes completely crazy at three in

5
00:00:08,412 --> 00:00:09,038
the morning.

6
00:00:09,674 --> 00:00:11,407
But every one of those weird habits

7
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:12,217
has a reason.

8
00:00:12,472 --> 00:00:14,943
Here are seven strange things cats do,

9
00:00:15,234 --> 00:00:16,919
and the science behind each one.

10
00:00:17,538 --> 00:00:18,153
Number one:

11
00:00:18,491 --> 00:00:19,546
making biscuits.

12
00:00:19,777 --> 00:00:21,569
When a cat pushes its paws in

13
00:00:21,431 --> 00:00:23,105
and out on a soft blanket, or

14
00:00:23,217 --> 00:00:24,557
on you, it's called kneading.

15
00:00:25,195 --> 00:00:27,655
It starts when cats are tiny kittens.

16
00:00:27,886 --> 00:00:30,095
Kneading their mother's belly helps the milk

17
00:00:29,945 --> 00:00:30,560
flow.

18
00:00:30,802 --> 00:00:32,405
So when an adult cat kneads you,

19
00:00:32,505 --> 00:00:34,810
it usually means it feels safe and

20
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:36,405
happy, like a kitten again.

21
00:00:37,062 --> 00:00:37,724
Number two:

22
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:38,843
the head bump.

23
00:00:39,074 --> 00:00:40,474
When your cat rubs its cheeks and

24
00:00:40,324 --> 00:00:42,117
forehead on you, it's not just being

25
00:00:41,967 --> 00:00:42,557
cute.

26
00:00:43,195 --> 00:00:45,071
Cats have scent glands on their face.

27
00:00:45,302 --> 00:00:48,536
Every rub leaves a tiny, invisible message

28
00:00:48,386 --> 00:00:49,583
on you that says:

29
00:00:49,874 --> 00:00:51,655
this human is part of my family.

30
00:00:52,295 --> 00:00:53,088
Number three:

31
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:54,469
the box obsession.

32
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:56,790
Give a cat an expensive bed, and

33
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:57,719
it will sit in the box it

34
00:00:57,569 --> 00:00:58,136
came in.

35
00:00:58,762 --> 00:01:01,460
Small, closed spaces make cats feel protected.

36
00:01:01,679 --> 00:01:03,638
In fact, a study with shelter cats

37
00:01:03,488 --> 00:01:04,579
found that the ones who got a

38
00:01:04,441 --> 00:01:06,817
hiding box calmed down faster than the

39
00:01:06,679 --> 00:01:07,496
ones who didn't.

40
00:01:08,128 --> 00:01:08,885
Number four:

41
00:01:09,235 --> 00:01:10,623
knocking things off the table.

42
00:01:10,843 --> 00:01:12,612
Your cat looks right at you, and

43
00:01:12,473 --> 00:01:14,421
slowly pushes your cup off the edge.

44
00:01:15,062 --> 00:01:16,831
Part of it is hunting instinct:

45
00:01:17,098 --> 00:01:19,212
tapping something to see if it moves,

46
00:01:19,074 --> 00:01:20,284
like testing prey.

47
00:01:20,515 --> 00:01:22,831
And part of it is pure strategy,

48
00:01:22,872 --> 00:01:25,057
because the second something falls, you look

49
00:01:24,907 --> 00:01:25,629
at your cat.

50
00:01:26,238 --> 00:01:27,055
Number five:

51
00:01:27,274 --> 00:01:28,007
the chattering.

52
00:01:28,238 --> 00:01:29,674
Your cat sees a bird through the

53
00:01:29,524 --> 00:01:32,138
window and starts making a weird clicking

54
00:01:31,988 --> 00:01:33,127
sound with its jaw.

55
00:01:33,728 --> 00:01:35,783
Scientists aren't completely sure why.

56
00:01:36,026 --> 00:01:38,283
It might be excitement, or frustration at

57
00:01:38,133 --> 00:01:39,343
prey it can't reach.

58
00:01:39,573 --> 00:01:42,402
And in the Amazon, researchers once saw

59
00:01:42,264 --> 00:01:44,390
a wild cat imitating the calls of

60
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:45,878
monkeys it was hunting.

61
00:01:46,471 --> 00:01:47,395
Number six:

62
00:01:47,471 --> 00:01:48,276
the gifts.

63
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:50,943
Your cat proudly drops a mouse, a

64
00:01:50,805 --> 00:01:52,526
bug or a lizard right at your

65
00:01:52,376 --> 00:01:52,907
feet.

66
00:01:53,528 --> 00:01:56,393
Cats are natural hunters, and they often

67
00:01:56,243 --> 00:01:58,273
bring prey back to a place where

68
00:01:58,135 --> 00:01:59,333
they feel safe.

69
00:01:59,564 --> 00:02:01,333
Your home is that place.

70
00:02:01,564 --> 00:02:03,976
One popular theory says your cat might

71
00:02:03,838 --> 00:02:05,762
even be trying to share, or teach

72
00:02:05,612 --> 00:02:06,202
you to hunt.

73
00:02:06,828 --> 00:02:08,550
So, as gross as it is, don't

74
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:09,431
scold your cat.

75
00:02:09,673 --> 00:02:11,038
In its mind, it just did something

76
00:02:10,888 --> 00:02:11,526
great.

77
00:02:12,162 --> 00:02:12,931
Number seven:

78
00:02:13,127 --> 00:02:14,134
the zoomies.

79
00:02:14,365 --> 00:02:16,753
Out of nowhere, your cat sprints across

80
00:02:16,603 --> 00:02:18,253
the house like it's being chased by

81
00:02:18,103 --> 00:02:18,824
a ghost.

82
00:02:19,462 --> 00:02:21,493
Scientists even have a name for it:

83
00:02:21,712 --> 00:02:23,934
frenetic random activity periods.

84
00:02:24,153 --> 00:02:26,207
It's a burst of stored energy, and

85
00:02:26,057 --> 00:02:27,779
since cats are most active at dusk

86
00:02:27,629 --> 00:02:29,791
and dawn, it often hits at night.

87
00:02:30,428 --> 00:02:32,388
Indoor cats can get the zoomies even

88
00:02:32,238 --> 00:02:34,376
more, because they don't spend their energy

89
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:34,840
hunting.

90
00:02:35,071 --> 00:02:36,804
A good play session before bed can

91
00:02:36,666 --> 00:02:38,090
help everyone sleep.

92
00:02:38,683 --> 00:02:40,012
So the next time your cat does

93
00:02:39,862 --> 00:02:41,726
something weird, remember:

94
00:02:42,052 --> 00:02:44,059
there's almost always a reason behind it.

95
00:02:44,707 --> 00:02:46,286
Which of these seven does your cat

96
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:47,071
do the most?

97
00:02:47,302 --> 00:02:48,750
Tell me the number in the comments.
